{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794865968977,"sessionID":"ses_e19b0e5bfffeS7sVTvhEMa7jvO","part":{"partID":"prt_1e659361b001Mnj85TMWeKy5WE","sessionID":"ses_e19b0e5bfffeS7sVTvhEMa7jvO","messageID":"msg_1e658ea6e001BDkHcC0YuRYaZT","type":"tool","id":"call_cbabaa8e01d3464aaa32dcd3","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":".skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-review/SKILL.md","limit":80},"output":"Read file .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-review/SKILL.md, lines 1-80\n1: ---\n2: name: sk-code-review\n3: description: \"Stack-agnostic code-review for sk-code: findings-first severity, security/correctness minimums, and surface evidence.\"\n4: allowed-tools: [Read, Bash, Grep, Glob, Write]\n5: version: 1.9.18.0\n6: metadata:\n7:   family: sk-code\n8: ---\n9: \n10: # sk-code-review Mode - Stack-Agnostic Findings-First Review\n11: \n12: Universal findings-first review baseline paired with `sk-code` surface standards evidence for the detected code surface.\n13: \n14: ## 1. WHEN TO USE\n15: \n16: ### Activation Triggers\n17: \n18: Use the `sk-code-review` mode (of the sk-code family) when:\n19: - A user asks for code review, PR review, or merge readiness.\n20: - A workflow dispatches `@review` for pre-commit or gate validation.\n21: - A user requests security/correctness risk analysis before merge.\n22: - A user wants severity-ranked findings with file:line evidence.\n23: \n24: ### Keyword Triggers\n25: \n26: `review`, `code review`, `pr review`, `audit`, `security review`, `request changes`, `findings`, `blocking issues`, `merge readiness`\n27: \n28: ### Use Cases\n29: \n30: 1. Review-only pass: findings-first output with no code edits.\n31: 2. Gate validation: a pass or fail that follows severity for orchestrated workflows, with any score advisory only.\n32: 3. Focused risk pass: security, concurrency, correctness, or removal-focused review.\n33: \n34: ### When NOT to Use\n35: \n36: - Feature implementation without review intent; load the surface packet (`sk-code-skilled`) that carries the implement doctrine.\n37: - Pure documentation editing where code behavior is not being assessed.\n38: - Git-only workflow tasks (branching, rebasing, commit hygiene) without code quality evaluation intent.\n39: - Applying review fixes after findings are accepted; load the surface packet (`sk-code-skilled`) that carries the implement doctrine.\n40: - Author-side quality gates before review; use `sk-code-quality`.\n41: - Root-cause debugging; use the surface's `workflow-debug.md` doctrine.\n42: - Verification evidence collection; use the surface's `workflow-verify.md` doctrine.\n43: \n44: ---\n45: \n46: ## 2. SMART ROUTING\n47: \n48: The hub bundles this mode for a code review, a pull-request review, merge readiness or a security and correctness pass over a diff.\n49: Its vocabulary is `review`, `code review`, `pr review`, `audit`, `security review`, `request changes`, `findings`, `blocking issues` and `merge readiness`.\n50: The first resource it loads is `references/review-core.md`, the doctrine both `@review` and `@deep-review` read.\n51: \n52: Review keeps no detection of its own.\n53: The quality runner's explain entry is the one resolver, and it applies the shared detection contract, [`stack-detection.md`](../shared/references/stack-detection.md) section 2.\n54: A surface the hub's router bundles from request words is only a candidate: it applies to the files the resolver places in it.\n55: \n56: The rule-override algorithm it hands to the review lives in [`review-core.md`](./references/review-core.md) section 9.\n57: Read [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting skill-local resources; open only the matched files.\n58: \n59: ---\n60: \n61: ## 3. HOW IT WORKS\n62: \n63: ### Phase 1: Scope and Baseline\n64: \n65: 1. Check the two efficiency gates in section 8 before scoping anything: M-1 skips a review whose pull request is unchanged since the last one, and M-2 skips a diff below its opt-in evidence threshold unless it touches a sensitive path. A skip ends the review there, with the status line alone.\n66: 2. Inspect the review target (`git diff`, staged diff, file list, or commit range).\n67: 3. Load baseline standards from the `sk-code-review` mode (of the sk-code family).\n68: 4. Resolve the rule sources for the reviewed files in one call, from the reviewed repository's root, where `<hub>` is the sk-code folder this mode was loaded from (`.skilled/skills/sk-code` in the repository that ships it):\n69: \n70:    ```bash\n71:    git diff --name-only --diff-filter=d <range>   # the reviewed paths that still exist\n72:    node <hub>/sk-code-quality/scripts/quality-gate.cjs --explain <file> [<file>...]\n73:    ```\n74: \n75:    Pass only paths that exist as regular files. One deleted or missing path makes the runner stop the whole call with `FILE_NOT_FOUND`, exit 3, which would drop every surface and override for the files that do exist.\n76: \n77:    - For a diff, `--diff-filter=d` leaves the deleted paths out.\n78:    - For a file list, drop any path that is not a regular file.\n79:    - List a deleted path on the `Not checked:` line when its removal matters to the review, such as a deleted test or a removed caller.\n80: \n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 81]","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":true},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-review/SKILL.md, lines 1-80\n1: ---\n2: name: sk-code-review\n3: description: \"Stack-agnostic code-review for sk-code: findings-first severity, security/correctness minimums, and surface evidence.\"\n4: allowed-tools: [Read, Bash, Grep, Glob, Write]\n5: version: 1.9.18.0\n6: metadata:\n7:   family: sk-code\n8: ---\n9: \n10: # sk-code-review Mode - Stack-Agnostic Findings-First Review\n11: \n12: Universal findings-first review baseline paired with `sk-code` surface standards evidence for the detected code surface.\n13: \n14: ## 1. WHEN TO USE\n15: \n16: ### Activation Triggers\n17: \n18: Use the `sk-code-review` mode (of the sk-code family) when:\n19: - A user asks for code review, PR review, or merge readiness.\n20: - A workflow dispatches `@review` for pre-commit or gate validation.\n21: - A user requests security/correctness risk analysis before merge.\n22: - A user wants severity-ranked findings with file:line evidence.\n23: \n24: ### Keyword Triggers\n25: \n26: `review`, `code review`, `pr review`, `audit`, `security review`, `request changes`, `findings`, `blocking issues`, `merge readiness`\n27: \n28: ### Use Cases\n29: \n30: 1. Review-only pass: findings-first output with no code edits.\n31: 2. Gate validation: a pass or fail that follows severity for orchestrated workflows, with any score advisory only.\n32: 3. Focused risk pass: security, concurrency, correctness, or removal-focused review.\n33: \n34: ### When NOT to Use\n35: \n36: - Feature implementation without review intent; load the surface packet (`sk-code-skilled`) that carries the implement doctrine.\n37: - Pure documentation editing where code behavior is not being assessed.\n38: - Git-only workflow tasks (branching, rebasing, commit hygiene) without code quality evaluation intent.\n39: - Applying review fixes after findings are accepted; load the surface packet (`sk-code-skilled`) that carries the implement doctrine.\n40: - Author-side quality gates before review; use `sk-code-quality`.\n41: - Root-cause debugging; use the surface's `workflow-debug.md` doctrine.\n42: - Verification evidence collection; use the surface's `workflow-verify.md` doctrine.\n43: \n44: ---\n45: \n46: ## 2. SMART ROUTING\n47: \n48: The hub bundles this mode for a code review, a pull-request review, merge readiness or a security and correctness pass over a diff.\n49: Its vocabulary is `review`, `code review`, `pr review`, `audit`, `security review`, `request changes`, `findings`, `blocking issues` and `merge readiness`.\n50: The first resource it loads is `references/review-core.md`, the doctrine both `@review` and `@deep-review` read.\n51: \n52: Review keeps no detection of its own.\n53: The quality runner's explain entry is the one resolver, and it applies the shared detection contract, [`stack-detection.md`](../shared/references/stack-detection.md) section 2.\n54: A surface the hub's router bundles from request words is only a candidate: it applies to the files the resolver places in it.\n55: \n56: The rule-override algorithm it hands to the review lives in [`review-core.md`](./references/review-core.md) section 9.\n57: Read [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting skill-local resources; open only the matched files.\n58: \n59: ---\n60: \n61: ## 3. HOW IT WORKS\n62: \n63: ### Phase 1: Scope and Baseline\n64: \n65: 1. Check the two efficiency gates in section 8 before scoping anything: M-1 skips a review whose pull request is unchanged since the last one, and M-2 skips a diff below its opt-in evidence threshold unless it touches a sensitive path. A skip ends the review there, with the status line alone.\n66: 2. Inspect the review target (`git diff`, staged diff, file list, or commit range).\n67: 3. Load baseline standards from the `sk-code-review` mode (of the sk-code family).\n68: 4. Resolve the rule sources for the reviewed files in one call, from the reviewed repository's root, where `<hub>` is the sk-code folder this mode was loaded from (`.skilled/skills/sk-code` in the repository that ships it):\n69: \n70:    ```bash\n71:    git diff --name-only --diff-filter=d <range>   # the reviewed paths that still exist\n72:    node <hub>/sk-code-quality/scripts/quality-gate.cjs --explain <file> [<file>...]\n73:    ```\n74: \n75:    Pass only paths that exist as regular files. One deleted or missing path makes the runner stop the whole call with `FILE_NOT_FOUND`, exit 3, which would drop every surface and override for the files that do exist.\n76: \n77:    - For a diff, `--diff-filter=d` leaves the deleted paths out.\n78:    - For a file list, drop any path that is not a regular file.\n79:    - List a deleted path on the `Not checked:` line when its removal matters to the review, such as a deleted test or a removed caller.\n80: \n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 81]"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\".skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-review/SKILL.md\",\"limit\":80}"},"time":{"start":1794865968667,"end":1794865968977}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794865969050,"sessionID":"ses_e19b0e5bfffeS7sVTvhEMa7jvO","part":{"partID":"prt_1e6592b8a001AFWzECdWCN8U6r","sessionID":"ses_e19b0e5bfffeS7sVTvhEMa7jvO","messageID":"msg_1e658ea6e001BDkHcC0YuRYaZT","type":"tool","id":"call_082ccd976de64dbaac3aaaa1","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research && node --input-type=module - <<'EOF'\nimport { attachCommand, commandOnlyMentioned } from './.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts';\nconst SINGLE = (m) => ({action:'route',selectionKind:'single',targets:[{workflowMode:m}]});\nconsole.log('F003 attach:', JSON.stringify(attachCommand('sk-doc', SINGLE('sk-create-readme'), 'do not run [/create:readme](https://example.com/docs) yet')));\nconsole.log('F004 attach:', JSON.stringify(attachCommand('sk-doc', SINGLE('sk-create-readme'), '/create:readme, what does it do?')));\nconst asked = new RegExp(`(?:^|[,.;!?\\\\n]) *(?:(?:and|then|so|please|now|just|go ahead and) )*(?:run|start|use|invoke|launch|execute|call|trigger|open|rerun|re-run|kick off) it\\\\b`);\nconst tail = ' command file, then run it';\nconsole.log('old-path pronoun regex on F001 tail:', asked.test(tail));\nconsole.log('old-path pronoun regex on F002 tail:', asked.test(\" and don't run it twice\"));\nEOF\nawk 'NR==435 || NR==425 || NR==436 { print NR\": \"length($0)\" chars\" }' .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts"},"output":"F003 attach: \"/create:readme\"\nF004 attach: \"/create:readme\"\nold-path pronoun regex on F001 tail: true\nold-path pronoun regex on F002 tail: false\n425: 80 chars\n435: 148 chars\n436: 48 chars\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"F003 attach: \"/create:readme\"\nF004 attach: \"/create:readme\"\nold-path pronoun regex on F001 tail: true\nold-path pronoun regex on F002 tail: false\n425: 80 chars\n435: 148 chars\n436: 48 chars\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\":\"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research && node --input-type=module - <<'EOF'\\nimport { attachCommand, commandOnlyMentioned } from './.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts';\\nconst SINGLE = (m) => ({action:'route',selectionKind:'single',targets:[{workflowMode:m}]});\\nconsole.log('F003 attach:', JSON.stringify(attachCommand('sk-doc', SINGLE('sk-create-readme'), 'do not run [/create:readme](https://example.com/docs) yet')));\\nconsole.log('F004 attach:', JSON.stringify(attachCommand('sk-doc', SINGLE('sk-create-readme'), '/create:readme, what does it do?')));\\nconst asked = new RegExp(`(?:^|[,.;!?\\\\\\\\n]) *(?:(?:and|then|so|please|now|just|go ahead and) )*(?:run|start|use|invoke|launch|execute|call|trigger|open|rerun|re-run|kick off) it\\\\\\\\b`);\\nconst tail = ' command file, then run it';\\nconsole.log('old-path pronoun regex on F001 tail:', asked.test(tail));\\nconsole.log('old-path pronoun regex on F002 tail:', asked.test(\\\" and don't run it twice\\\"));\\nEOF\\nawk 'NR==435 || NR==425 || NR==436 { print NR\\\": \\\"length($0)\\\" chars\\\" }' .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts\"}"},"time":{"start":1794865965962,"end":1794865969050}}}}
