{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794866033378,"sessionID":"ses_e19b0e5bfffeS7sVTvhEMa7jvO","part":{"partID":"prt_1e6597a6f001R0cbcziaAf5ZOS","sessionID":"ses_e19b0e5bfffeS7sVTvhEMa7jvO","messageID":"msg_1e65954ab001mh9JtaGcctaIuY","type":"tool","id":"call_2f7e7af3183445d2841986b5","tool":"write","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"# Review Iteration 001 - mention-13\n\n## Dispatcher\n\n- Mode: review. Target agent: `@deep-review`, executed in-process by this detached fan-out lineage (no nested dispatch).\n- Session: `fanout-mention-13-1794865302020-qunumz`, generation 1, lineage `mention-13` under `specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/lineages/mention-13`.\n- Iteration: 1 of 8. Dimension: correctness. Focus: the two new tail rules and the widened occurrence boundary in `commandOnlyMentioned`, plus the new typed start boundary.\n- Operator review focus, and how this iteration addressed it: pass thirteen's brief asks for a request that now reads as a mention, and for a refusal or question shape still read as a request. This iteration read `review-brief-13.txt` and `review-scope-13.txt` first, diffed the two mention scope files between `b8ebee37d75` and `932aff4e437`, read both whole files, and then ran the module's exported guards over 34 concrete prompts. It found two request shapes that now read as mentions (F001, F002) and two refusal/question shapes still read as requests (F003, F004).\n\n## Files Reviewed\n\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:1-566` (whole file at `932aff4e437`; working tree bytes match that commit)\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:197-245` (typed reader, focused reread)\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:359-476` (lead lists, new tail rules, mention function, focused reread)\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-attach-guards.vitest.ts:1-615` (whole file at `932aff4e437`)\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-attach-guards.vitest.ts:138-320` (mention suite, focused reread)\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-attach-guards.vitest.ts:509-597` (typed boundary suite, focused reread)\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/scorer/fusion.ts:1200-1216` (`withholdMentionedCommands`, the consumer)\n- `specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/review-brief-13.txt:1-10`\n- `specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/review-scope-13.txt:1-6`\n- `specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/040-final-alignment-loop/spec.md:40-202`\n- `specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/040-final-alignment-loop/tasks.md:37-46,130-131`\n- `specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/040-final-alignment-loop/acceptance-criteria.md:56`\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/manual-testing-playbook/native-cli-tools/command-offer-fit-gates.md:3-123`\n\n## Findings - New\n\n### P0 Findings\n\nNone. No input tested produces data loss, an executed command the reader did not type, or an authorization change; every failure here is a withheld or a wrongly offered command. `commandOnlyMentioned` is a filter over an offer, so its worst case is a lost offer, not an unsafe run: `attachCommand` returns `undefined` and `withholdMentionedCommands` clears a command row, and the reader can still run the command by hand.\n\n### P1 Findings\n\n1. **A document naming returns a mention before a later request clause is read** -- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:458` -- `COMMAND_AS_DOCUMENT` returns `true` from `commandOnlyMentioned` on the document noun alone, so the `COMMAND_ASKED_BY_PRONOUN` check at line 460 never runs for that occurrence. `open the /deep:review command file, then run it` reads as a mention, and `typedCommandForTop('system-deep-loop', ...)` returns `undefined` for a prompt that literally asks to run the command. The same shadowing withholds a hub command the reader asked for: `attachCommand('system-deep-loop', SINGLE('review'), 'read the /deep:review docs, then do a thorough review of this diff')` returns `undefined` although `DEPTH_REQUEST` matches `thorough` at line 323. The function's own contract at lines 403-407 says a clause that opens with a use verb and `it` turns an earlier naming into a request, and the diff placed the new early return ahead of it.\n   Finding class: logic-ordering\n   Scope proof: Ran the exported `commandOnlyMentioned` and `typedCommandForTop` on the two prompts above; both classify as mentions. The old tree had no `COMMAND_AS_DOCUMENT` return, and the unchanged pronoun regex matches the tail `command file, then run it`, so the earlier read was a request. `REQUEST6`/`CONTROL5` do not carry a document naming, which is why the suite stays green.\n   Affected surface hints: `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts`, `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/scorer/fusion.ts`, `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-attach-guards.vitest.ts`\n   Case: `open the /deep:review command file, then run it` typed by a reader who wants the review run, and `read the /deep:review docs, then do a thorough review of this diff` on a hub route to the review mode.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"type\": \"claim-adjudication\",\n  \"findingId\": \"F001\",\n  \"claim\": \"The new document rule returns a mention before the existing pronoun-request rule can read a later request, so a prompt that asks to run the command loses it.\",\n  \"evidenceRefs\": [\n    \".skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:458\",\n    \".skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:460\",\n    \".skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:403-407\",\n    \".skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:527\"\n  ],\n  \"counterevidenceSought\": \"Checked whether the determiner requirement or the tail split already excludes these prompts. It does not: `the /deep:review` satisfies the determiner test at line 458, and the tail starts with ` command file`, which `COMMAND_AS_DOCUMENT` accepts. Checked whether the deep-loop depth gate rescues the hub case; it is consulted after the mention check at line 527, so it never runs.\",\n  \"alternativeExplanation\": \"A reader naming the command file may be assumed not to want the run. Rejected: both prompts carry an explicit later request (`then run it`, `do a thorough review`), and the function's own comment says that clause wins.\",\n  \"finalSeverity\": \"P1\",\n  \"confidence\": 0.9,\n  \"downgradeTrigger\": \"Downgrade to P2 if a follow-up shows no caller reaches `commandOnlyMentioned` with a document naming plus a later request, or if the document rule is moved after the pronoun-request check and the two prompts then read as requests.\"\n}\n```\n\n2. **A limiting negation after the name reads as a refusal** -- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:413` -- `COMMAND_DECLINED_BY_PRONOUN` ends at `it\\b`, so any qualifier after `it` still satisfies the refusal: `run /speckit:save and don't run it twice` reads as a mention, `typedCommandForTop('system-spec-kit', ...)` returns `undefined`, and `run /deep:review but never run it without a budget` does the same on the hub path. The comment at lines 409-412 claims the rule keeps a request a request, and the packet records this exact risk as High at `spec.md:150` with `REQUEST6`, `CONTROL5` and the wrapper matrix as the mitigation; none of those rows carries a limiting clause after `it`, so the mitigation does not cover the shape it names.\n   Finding class: logic-overreach\n   Scope proof: Ran the exported guards on both prompts; both classify as mentions and `typedCommandForTop` drops the typed command. The unchanged lead tests cannot produce this reading: the only decline lead before the name would need `do not run /speckit:save`, and the bare-ask lead is false, so on `b8ebee37d75` the prompt returned a request.\n   Affected surface hints: `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts`, `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/scorer/fusion.ts`, `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-attach-guards.vitest.ts`\n   Case: `run /speckit:save and don't run it twice`, and `run /deep:review but never run it without a budget`, both typed requests with a limit rather than a refusal.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"type\": \"claim-adjudication\",\n  \"findingId\": \"F002\",\n  \"claim\": \"The tail refusal matches a negated use verb plus `it` without bounding what follows, so a request that limits how the command runs is read as a refusal and the command is dropped.\",\n  \"evidenceRefs\": [\n    \".skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:413\",\n    \".skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:459\",\n    \"specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/040-final-alignment-loop/spec.md:150\",\n    \".skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-attach-guards.vitest.ts:58-73\"\n  ],\n  \"counterevidenceSought\": \"Searched the fixtures for a limiting clause after `it`: `REQUEST6` has `don't forget to run`, `CONTROL5` has `never mind just run`, and the one negative tail row is `don't skip it`. None exercises `don't run it <qualifier>`, so no existing row contradicts the reproduction. Checked the `why not` guard at line 367; it does not apply to `don't`.\",\n  \"alternativeExplanation\": \"`don't run it twice` could be read as refusing the second run, which is still a refusal of a run. Rejected as the intended reading here: the clause limits a run the reader already asked for, and the function's stated job is to keep a request a request.\",\n  \"finalSeverity\": \"P1\",\n  \"confidence\": 0.85,\n  \"downgradeTrigger\": \"Downgrade to P2 if the tail rule is narrowed to refuse only a clause that ends at `it` or continues with a sentence boundary, and these prompts then read as requests.\"\n}\n```\n\n### P2 Findings\n\n1. **A markdown link label abuts its target and the naming stops being an occurrence** -- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:441` -- the fold drops `[`, `]`, `(` and `)`, so `[/create:readme](https://example.com/docs)` becomes `/create:readmehttps://example.com/docs`; the lookahead added at line 451 (`(?![a-z0-9_-]|[./][a-z0-9])`) then rejects the name because it is followed by `h`, `occurrences.length` is 0 at line 452, and the function returns `false`. `do not run [/create:readme](https://example.com/docs) yet` therefore reads as a request and `attachCommand('sk-doc', SINGLE('sk-create-readme'), ...)` returns `/create:readme`. The wrapper matrix pins `[name]` with nothing after the closing bracket, which is the one form where the fold is safe.\n   Finding class: normalization-edge\n   Scope proof: Probed the exported guard and `attachCommand` with the linked label, a parenthesized aside with no space (`/create:readme(see note below)`) and the bracketed form the matrix covers; only the merged forms flip to request. `withholdMentionedCommands` at `fusion.ts:1213` keeps the command row on the same reading.\n   Affected surface hints: `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts`, `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/scorer/fusion.ts`, `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-attach-guards.vitest.ts`\n   Case: `do not run [/create:readme](https://example.com/docs) yet`, and `do not run /create:readme(see note below)`, both refusals written in chat markdown.\n\n2. **The post-name reader covers one clause shape, so a question or a sibling refusal after the name still reads as a request** -- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:459` -- the new rule reads `(do not|don't|dont|never) <use verb> it` after the name, while the ask rule at line 460 requires the clause to open with a use verb. `COMMAND_ASK_LEADS` only matches before the name, so `/create:readme, what does it do?` reads as a request and `attachCommand` offers `/create:readme`, and a refusal that uses a lead from `COMMAND_DECLINE_LEADS` other than the four negations, such as `skip` at line 376, is missed too: `/create:readme is wrong, skip it` reads as a request.\n   Finding class: vocabulary-asymmetry\n   Scope proof: Probed four post-name shapes: a question, `skip it`, `won't run it` and `no need to run it`; all four return `false`, and the first two offer the command through `attachCommand`. The pre-name lists already treat `skip` and `won't` as refusals, so the asymmetry is inside the file rather than an unclaimed vocabulary.\n   Affected surface hints: `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts`, `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-attach-guards.vitest.ts`\n   Case: `/create:readme, what does it do?` asked to decide whether to run it, and `/create:readme is wrong, skip it` refusing it after naming it.\n\n## Traceability Checks\n\n| Protocol | Status | Evidence |\n|----------|--------|----------|\n| `spec_code` | partial | `spec.md:57` names four refusal shapes and one behaviour claim (`a request still is`); the four refusal fixtures do read as mentions, and F001/F002 are the request direction failing. `spec.md:86` claims brackets, `~~`, `<code>`, guillemets and primes are dropped; lines 440-441 drop exactly that set, and the matrix at `command-attach-guards.vitest.ts:175-178` lists the same twelve pairs. |\n| `checklist_evidence` | notApplicable | `tasks.md:45` records 16 failing-first cases for this branch; this pass reviews the behaviour, not the packet's task closure, and `040-final-alignment-loop` is still in progress. |\n| `skill_agent` | pass | No agent definition is in the diff; both scope files are runtime library and test files. |\n| `agent_cross_runtime` | notApplicable | No runtime mirror is touched by the two scope files. |\n| `feature_catalog_code` | partial | The guards test pins the twelve wrapper pairs at `command-attach-guards.vitest.ts:175-189`; F001, F002, F003 and F004 are the shapes it cannot see. |\n| `playbook_capability` | partial | Read `command-offer-fit-gates.md:22-24,51,68-72` for the refusal and question contract `NC-012` pins; no playbook scenario was executed, and the two guards were run directly instead. |\n\n## Integration Evidence\n\n- `attachCommand` (`.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:527`) consults `commandOnlyMentioned` before the deep-loop depth gate at line 545, so a false mention wins over depth wording. Reproduced on the hub path for the review mode.\n- `withholdMentionedCommands` (`.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/scorer/fusion.ts:1213`) clears `passes_threshold` on a command row when the guard returns true, so F002 and F003 reach the ranked recommendation list as well as the hub offer.\n- `typedCommandForTop` (`.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:259`) drops a typed command on a mention, so F001 and F002 cost the reader the command they typed.\n\n## Edge Cases\n\n- Ambiguity: `don't run it twice` (F002) is the one place where a reader could argue the negation is a refusal. It is recorded at P1 with a downgrade trigger rather than assumed; the mirror prompt `don't skip it`, which the packet pins at `spec.md:178`, still reads as a request.\n- Contradiction: `spec.md:72` records the bracket case as `= false (plain form true)`. That line is pre-fix evidence for the defect this branch closes; the current tree returns `true`, so the line is historical and not a defect.\n- Partial success: this iteration covered correctness only. Security, traceability and maintainability remain for later iterations, so the run is not complete.\n- Recorded limits respected: the `:auto.md` suffix, the `node -e` arrow, `isolation-check.sh`, underscore emphasis and the open P2 lists were not re-reported; none of the four findings restates them.\n\n## Confirmed-Clean Surfaces\n\n- The twelve wrapper pairs and the retired name read every `PLAIN_ROWS` fixture the same as plain: re-ran all 21 rows across the twelve pairs and the retired spelling, 0 mismatches.\n- `REFUSE8` (8/8), `QUESTION2` (2/2) still read as mentions and `REQUEST6` (6/6), `CONTROL5` (5/5) still read as requests on the current tree.\n- The doc path is skipped: `do not run /create:readme, read /create:readme.md` reads as a mention, and `typedCommandIn('open /create:readme/notes.txt')` is still `undefined`.\n- The typed start boundary accepts the new marks: `run **/deep:review** now`, `[..]`, curly quotes and `<code>` all return `/deep:review`; `see /create:sk-skill.md` stays `undefined`.\n\n## Ruled Out\n\n- A missing occurrence for the bracketed form: the scan finds the name inside `[...]` because the fold drops the brackets; the failure in F003 is the merge with a following target, not the brackets themselves.\n- A regression in `typedCommandIn` from the widened start boundary: probed `*/`, `~/`, `>/`, `[/`, `(/`, guillemet and prime prefixes; every hit is a wrapped command, and no path or glob form newly reads as a typed command.\n- A new false offer from the decline rule before the name: probed `not sure, use /create:readme`, `never mind just run /speckit:save`, `dont guess, run /speckit:plan` and `without asking, run /speckit:complete`; all still read as requests.\n- A security or ReDoS surface in the two new regexes: both are single alternations with no nested quantifier over the same text, `COMMAND_AS_DOCUMENT` is anchored, and the module reads only its arguments (see iteration 3).\n\n## Next Focus\n\n- Dimension: traceability.\n- Focus area: `spec.md` §3 and REQ-001 against the determiner requirement and the wrapper matrix, plus `CHK-FIX-001`/`CHK-FIX-002` evidence rows.\n- Reason: correctness produced the two P1s; the packet's own scope bullet states the document shape without the determiner the code requires, which needs a traceability ruling rather than a second correctness finding.\n- Rotation status: correctness complete, traceability next, then security and maintainability.\n- Blocked/productive carry-forward: productive: exported-function probes with concrete prompts; every later finding gets the same treatment.\n- Required evidence: file:line in `spec.md`, `tasks.md` and `acceptance-criteria.md`, plus the probe result for each claim.\n- Recovery note: none.\n\nReview verdict: CONDITIONAL\n","path":"specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/lineages/mention-13/iterations/iteration-001.md"},"output":"Created file successfully: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/lineages/mention-13/iterations/iteration-001.md","title":"write","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Created file successfully: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/lineages/mention-13/iterations/iteration-001.md"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"content\":\"# Review Iteration 001 - mention-13\\n\\n## Dispatcher\\n\\n- Mode: review. Target agent: `@deep-review`, executed in-process by this detached fan-out lineage (no nested dispatch).\\n- Session: `fanout-mention-13-1794865302020-qunumz`, generation 1, lineage `mention-13` under `specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/lineages/mention-13`.\\n- Iteration: 1 of 8. Dimension: correctness. Focus: the two new tail rules and the widened occurrence boundary in `commandOnlyMentioned`, plus the new typed start boundary.\\n- Operator review focus, and how this iteration addressed it: pass thirteen's brief asks for a request that now reads as a mention, and for a refusal or question shape still read as a request. This iteration read `review-brief-13.txt` and `review-scope-13.txt` first, diffed the two mention scope files between `b8ebee37d75` and `932aff4e437`, read both whole files, and then ran the module's exported guards over 34 concrete prompts. It found two request shapes that now read as mentions (F001, F002) and two refusal/question shapes still read as requests (F003, F004).\\n\\n## Files Reviewed\\n\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:1-566` (whole file at `932aff4e437`; working tree bytes match that commit)\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:197-245` (typed reader, focused reread)\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:359-476` (lead lists, new tail rules, mention function, focused reread)\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-attach-guards.vitest.ts:1-615` (whole file at `932aff4e437`)\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-attach-guards.vitest.ts:138-320` (mention suite, focused reread)\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-attach-guards.vitest.ts:509-597` (typed boundary suite, focused reread)\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/scorer/fusion.ts:1200-1216` (`withholdMentionedCommands`, the consumer)\\n- `specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/review-brief-13.txt:1-10`\\n- `specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/review-scope-13.txt:1-6`\\n- `specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/040-final-alignment-loop/spec.md:40-202`\\n- `specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/040-final-alignment-loop/tasks.md:37-46,130-131`\\n- `specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/040-final-alignment-loop/acceptance-criteria.md:56`\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/manual-testing-playbook/native-cli-tools/command-offer-fit-gates.md:3-123`\\n\\n## Findings - New\\n\\n### P0 Findings\\n\\nNone. No input tested produces data loss, an executed command the reader did not type, or an authorization change; every failure here is a withheld or a wrongly offered command. `commandOnlyMentioned` is a filter over an offer, so its worst case is a lost offer, not an unsafe run: `attachCommand` returns `undefined` and `withholdMentionedCommands` clears a command row, and the reader can still run the command by hand.\\n\\n### P1 Findings\\n\\n1. **A document naming returns a mention before a later request clause is read** -- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:458` -- `COMMAND_AS_DOCUMENT` returns `true` from `commandOnlyMentioned` on the document noun alone, so the `COMMAND_ASKED_BY_PRONOUN` check at line 460 never runs for that occurrence. `open the /deep:review command file, then run it` reads as a mention, and `typedCommandForTop('system-deep-loop', ...)` returns `undefined` for a prompt that literally asks to run the command. The same shadowing withholds a hub command the reader asked for: `attachCommand('system-deep-loop', SINGLE('review'), 'read the /deep:review docs, then do a thorough review of this diff')` returns `undefined` although `DEPTH_REQUEST` matches `thorough` at line 323. The function's own contract at lines 403-407 says a clause that opens with a use verb and `it` turns an earlier naming into a request, and the diff placed the new early return ahead of it.\\n   Finding class: logic-ordering\\n   Scope proof: Ran the exported `commandOnlyMentioned` and `typedCommandForTop` on the two prompts above; both classify as mentions. The old tree had no `COMMAND_AS_DOCUMENT` return, and the unchanged pronoun regex matches the tail `command file, then run it`, so the earlier read was a request. `REQUEST6`/`CONTROL5` do not carry a document naming, which is why the suite stays green.\\n   Affected surface hints: `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts`, `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/scorer/fusion.ts`, `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-attach-guards.vitest.ts`\\n   Case: `open the /deep:review command file, then run it` typed by a reader who wants the review run, and `read the /deep:review docs, then do a thorough review of this diff` on a hub route to the review mode.\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"type\\\": \\\"claim-adjudication\\\",\\n  \\\"findingId\\\": \\\"F001\\\",\\n  \\\"claim\\\": \\\"The new document rule returns a mention before the existing pronoun-request rule can read a later request, so a prompt that asks to run the command loses it.\\\",\\n  \\\"evidenceRefs\\\": [\\n    \\\".skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:458\\\",\\n    \\\".skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:460\\\",\\n    \\\".skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:403-407\\\",\\n    \\\".skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:527\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"counterevidenceSought\\\": \\\"Checked whether the determiner requirement or the tail split already excludes these prompts. It does not: `the /deep:review` satisfies the determiner test at line 458, and the tail starts with ` command file`, which `COMMAND_AS_DOCUMENT` accepts. Checked whether the deep-loop depth gate rescues the hub case; it is consulted after the mention check at line 527, so it never runs.\\\",\\n  \\\"alternativeExplanation\\\": \\\"A reader naming the command file may be assumed not to want the run. Rejected: both prompts carry an explicit later request (`then run it`, `do a thorough review`), and the function's own comment says that clause wins.\\\",\\n  \\\"finalSeverity\\\": \\\"P1\\\",\\n  \\\"confidence\\\": 0.9,\\n  \\\"downgradeTrigger\\\": \\\"Downgrade to P2 if a follow-up shows no caller reaches `commandOnlyMentioned` with a document naming plus a later request, or if the document rule is moved after the pronoun-request check and the two prompts then read as requests.\\\"\\n}\\n```\\n\\n2. **A limiting negation after the name reads as a refusal** -- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:413` -- `COMMAND_DECLINED_BY_PRONOUN` ends at `it\\\\b`, so any qualifier after `it` still satisfies the refusal: `run /speckit:save and don't run it twice` reads as a mention, `typedCommandForTop('system-spec-kit', ...)` returns `undefined`, and `run /deep:review but never run it without a budget` does the same on the hub path. The comment at lines 409-412 claims the rule keeps a request a request, and the packet records this exact risk as High at `spec.md:150` with `REQUEST6`, `CONTROL5` and the wrapper matrix as the mitigation; none of those rows carries a limiting clause after `it`, so the mitigation does not cover the shape it names.\\n   Finding class: logic-overreach\\n   Scope proof: Ran the exported guards on both prompts; both classify as mentions and `typedCommandForTop` drops the typed command. The unchanged lead tests cannot produce this reading: the only decline lead before the name would need `do not run /speckit:save`, and the bare-ask lead is false, so on `b8ebee37d75` the prompt returned a request.\\n   Affected surface hints: `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts`, `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/scorer/fusion.ts`, `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-attach-guards.vitest.ts`\\n   Case: `run /speckit:save and don't run it twice`, and `run /deep:review but never run it without a budget`, both typed requests with a limit rather than a refusal.\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"type\\\": \\\"claim-adjudication\\\",\\n  \\\"findingId\\\": \\\"F002\\\",\\n  \\\"claim\\\": \\\"The tail refusal matches a negated use verb plus `it` without bounding what follows, so a request that limits how the command runs is read as a refusal and the command is dropped.\\\",\\n  \\\"evidenceRefs\\\": [\\n    \\\".skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:413\\\",\\n    \\\".skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:459\\\",\\n    \\\"specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/040-final-alignment-loop/spec.md:150\\\",\\n    \\\".skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-attach-guards.vitest.ts:58-73\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"counterevidenceSought\\\": \\\"Searched the fixtures for a limiting clause after `it`: `REQUEST6` has `don't forget to run`, `CONTROL5` has `never mind just run`, and the one negative tail row is `don't skip it`. None exercises `don't run it <qualifier>`, so no existing row contradicts the reproduction. Checked the `why not` guard at line 367; it does not apply to `don't`.\\\",\\n  \\\"alternativeExplanation\\\": \\\"`don't run it twice` could be read as refusing the second run, which is still a refusal of a run. Rejected as the intended reading here: the clause limits a run the reader already asked for, and the function's stated job is to keep a request a request.\\\",\\n  \\\"finalSeverity\\\": \\\"P1\\\",\\n  \\\"confidence\\\": 0.85,\\n  \\\"downgradeTrigger\\\": \\\"Downgrade to P2 if the tail rule is narrowed to refuse only a clause that ends at `it` or continues with a sentence boundary, and these prompts then read as requests.\\\"\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### P2 Findings\\n\\n1. **A markdown link label abuts its target and the naming stops being an occurrence** -- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:441` -- the fold drops `[`, `]`, `(` and `)`, so `[/create:readme](https://example.com/docs)` becomes `/create:readmehttps://example.com/docs`; the lookahead added at line 451 (`(?![a-z0-9_-]|[./][a-z0-9])`) then rejects the name because it is followed by `h`, `occurrences.length` is 0 at line 452, and the function returns `false`. `do not run [/create:readme](https://example.com/docs) yet` therefore reads as a request and `attachCommand('sk-doc', SINGLE('sk-create-readme'), ...)` returns `/create:readme`. The wrapper matrix pins `[name]` with nothing after the closing bracket, which is the one form where the fold is safe.\\n   Finding class: normalization-edge\\n   Scope proof: Probed the exported guard and `attachCommand` with the linked label, a parenthesized aside with no space (`/create:readme(see note below)`) and the bracketed form the matrix covers; only the merged forms flip to request. `withholdMentionedCommands` at `fusion.ts:1213` keeps the command row on the same reading.\\n   Affected surface hints: `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts`, `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/scorer/fusion.ts`, `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-attach-guards.vitest.ts`\\n   Case: `do not run [/create:readme](https://example.com/docs) yet`, and `do not run /create:readme(see note below)`, both refusals written in chat markdown.\\n\\n2. **The post-name reader covers one clause shape, so a question or a sibling refusal after the name still reads as a request** -- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:459` -- the new rule reads `(do not|don't|dont|never) <use verb> it` after the name, while the ask rule at line 460 requires the clause to open with a use verb. `COMMAND_ASK_LEADS` only matches before the name, so `/create:readme, what does it do?` reads as a request and `attachCommand` offers `/create:readme`, and a refusal that uses a lead from `COMMAND_DECLINE_LEADS` other than the four negations, such as `skip` at line 376, is missed too: `/create:readme is wrong, skip it` reads as a request.\\n   Finding class: vocabulary-asymmetry\\n   Scope proof: Probed four post-name shapes: a question, `skip it`, `won't run it` and `no need to run it`; all four return `false`, and the first two offer the command through `attachCommand`. The pre-name lists already treat `skip` and `won't` as refusals, so the asymmetry is inside the file rather than an unclaimed vocabulary.\\n   Affected surface hints: `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts`, `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-attach-guards.vitest.ts`\\n   Case: `/create:readme, what does it do?` asked to decide whether to run it, and `/create:readme is wrong, skip it` refusing it after naming it.\\n\\n## Traceability Checks\\n\\n| Protocol | Status | Evidence |\\n|----------|--------|----------|\\n| `spec_code` | partial | `spec.md:57` names four refusal shapes and one behaviour claim (`a request still is`); the four refusal fixtures do read as mentions, and F001/F002 are the request direction failing. `spec.md:86` claims brackets, `~~`, `<code>`, guillemets and primes are dropped; lines 440-441 drop exactly that set, and the matrix at `command-attach-guards.vitest.ts:175-178` lists the same twelve pairs. |\\n| `checklist_evidence` | notApplicable | `tasks.md:45` records 16 failing-first cases for this branch; this pass reviews the behaviour, not the packet's task closure, and `040-final-alignment-loop` is still in progress. |\\n| `skill_agent` | pass | No agent definition is in the diff; both scope files are runtime library and test files. |\\n| `agent_cross_runtime` | notApplicable | No runtime mirror is touched by the two scope files. |\\n| `feature_catalog_code` | partial | The guards test pins the twelve wrapper pairs at `command-attach-guards.vitest.ts:175-189`; F001, F002, F003 and F004 are the shapes it cannot see. |\\n| `playbook_capability` | partial | Read `command-offer-fit-gates.md:22-24,51,68-72` for the refusal and question contract `NC-012` pins; no playbook scenario was executed, and the two guards were run directly instead. |\\n\\n## Integration Evidence\\n\\n- `attachCommand` (`.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:527`) consults `commandOnlyMentioned` before the deep-loop depth gate at line 545, so a false mention wins over depth wording. Reproduced on the hub path for the review mode.\\n- `withholdMentionedCommands` (`.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/scorer/fusion.ts:1213`) clears `passes_threshold` on a command row when the guard returns true, so F002 and F003 reach the ranked recommendation list as well as the hub offer.\\n- `typedCommandForTop` (`.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:259`) drops a typed command on a mention, so F001 and F002 cost the reader the command they typed.\\n\\n## Edge Cases\\n\\n- Ambiguity: `don't run it twice` (F002) is the one place where a reader could argue the negation is a refusal. It is recorded at P1 with a downgrade trigger rather than assumed; the mirror prompt `don't skip it`, which the packet pins at `spec.md:178`, still reads as a request.\\n- Contradiction: `spec.md:72` records the bracket case as `= false (plain form true)`. That line is pre-fix evidence for the defect this branch closes; the current tree returns `true`, so the line is historical and not a defect.\\n- Partial success: this iteration covered correctness only. Security, traceability and maintainability remain for later iterations, so the run is not complete.\\n- Recorded limits respected: the `:auto.md` suffix, the `node -e` arrow, `isolation-check.sh`, underscore emphasis and the open P2 lists were not re-reported; none of the four findings restates them.\\n\\n## Confirmed-Clean Surfaces\\n\\n- The twelve wrapper pairs and the retired name read every `PLAIN_ROWS` fixture the same as plain: re-ran all 21 rows across the twelve pairs and the retired spelling, 0 mismatches.\\n- `REFUSE8` (8/8), `QUESTION2` (2/2) still read as mentions and `REQUEST6` (6/6), `CONTROL5` (5/5) still read as requests on the current tree.\\n- The doc path is skipped: `do not run /create:readme, read /create:readme.md` reads as a mention, and `typedCommandIn('open /create:readme/notes.txt')` is still `undefined`.\\n- The typed start boundary accepts the new marks: `run **/deep:review** now`, `[..]`, curly quotes and `<code>` all return `/deep:review`; `see /create:sk-skill.md` stays `undefined`.\\n\\n## Ruled Out\\n\\n- A missing occurrence for the bracketed form: the scan finds the name inside `[...]` because the fold drops the brackets; the failure in F003 is the merge with a following target, not the brackets themselves.\\n- A regression in `typedCommandIn` from the widened start boundary: probed `*/`, `~/`, `>/`, `[/`, `(/`, guillemet and prime prefixes; every hit is a wrapped command, and no path or glob form newly reads as a typed command.\\n- A new false offer from the decline rule before the name: probed `not sure, use /create:readme`, `never mind just run /speckit:save`, `dont guess, run /speckit:plan` and `without asking, run /speckit:complete`; all still read as requests.\\n- A security or ReDoS surface in the two new regexes: both are single alternations with no nested quantifier over the same text, `COMMAND_AS_DOCUMENT` is anchored, and the module reads only its arguments (see iteration 3).\\n\\n## Next Focus\\n\\n- Dimension: traceability.\\n- Focus area: `spec.md` §3 and REQ-001 against the determiner requirement and the wrapper matrix, plus `CHK-FIX-001`/`CHK-FIX-002` evidence rows.\\n- Reason: correctness produced the two P1s; the packet's own scope bullet states the document shape without the determiner the code requires, which needs a traceability ruling rather than a second correctness finding.\\n- Rotation status: correctness complete, traceability next, then security and maintainability.\\n- Blocked/productive carry-forward: productive: exported-function probes with concrete prompts; every later finding gets the same treatment.\\n- Required evidence: file:line in `spec.md`, `tasks.md` and `acceptance-criteria.md`, plus the probe result for each claim.\\n- Recovery note: none.\\n\\nReview verdict: CONDITIONAL\\n\",\"path\":\"specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/lineages/mention-13/iterations/iteration-001.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794865986159,"end":1794866033378}}}}
