{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794866444457,"sessionID":"ses_e19b0e5bfffeS7sVTvhEMa7jvO","part":{"partID":"prt_1e66038f10026XZLphwgPMc4l2","sessionID":"ses_e19b0e5bfffeS7sVTvhEMa7jvO","messageID":"msg_1e65ffcfd001o45sMVP8Agh4pw","type":"tool","id":"call_fac09c7e35b64a3cb8acceba","tool":"write","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"# Review Iteration 003 - mention-13\n\n## Dispatcher\n\n- Mode: review. Target agent: `@deep-review`, executed in-process by this detached fan-out lineage (no nested dispatch).\n- Session: `fanout-mention-13-1794865302020-qunumz`, generation 1, lineage `mention-13`.\n- Iteration: 3 of 8. Dimension: security. Focus: the trust boundary of the prompt text, the two new patterns, and the module's import, environment and secret surface.\n- Operator review focus, and how this iteration addressed it: pass thirteen's brief asks whether a request now reads as a mention and whether a refusal or question still reads as a request. This iteration asked the same questions from the trust side: whether a crafted prompt can make the guard offer a command it would not otherwise offer, or can make it spend unbounded work, and whether the widened fold opens an injection path. It found no security defect; the evidence is recorded below so the clean result is a checked result rather than an assumption.\n\n## Files Reviewed\n\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:1-14` (module header, import surface)\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:197-245` (typed reader and its boundary constants)\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:316-476` (escaping, pattern construction, fold, occurrence scan)\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-attach-guards.vitest.ts:599-615` (generated-table contract tests)\n\n## Findings - New\n\n### P0 Findings\n\nNone.\n\n### P1 Findings\n\nNone new. F001 and F002 from iteration 1 remain active and are carried forward.\n\n### P2 Findings\n\nNone new.\n\n## Traceability Checks\n\n| Protocol | Status | Evidence |\n|----------|--------|----------|\n| `spec_code` | partial | Unchanged from iteration 2: the document bullet is narrower in code (F005). The security-relevant scope claim at `spec.md:165` (no holdout text read) was respected: this iteration read only source and test files. |\n| `checklist_evidence` | partial | Unchanged from iteration 2: `CHK-FIX-002` overstates matrix coverage (F006). `NFR-P01` at `spec.md:162` claims a constant number of regex passes for the fold, which the fold satisfies: five `.replace` calls plus four single-pass regexes, none of them looped. |\n| `skill_agent` | notApplicable | No agent definition in scope for this dimension. |\n| `agent_cross_runtime` | notApplicable | No runtime mirror is touched. |\n| `feature_catalog_code` | notApplicable | No feature catalog row covers a prompt-normalization security property. |\n| `playbook_capability` | notApplicable | No playbook scenario exists for a security property of the mention guard. |\n\n## Integration Evidence\n\nNone this iteration: the trust-boundary review stayed inside the module and its consumer contract already cited in iteration 1.\n\n## Edge Cases\n\n- Ambiguity: whether a prompt that suppresses its own command offer is a security issue. It is not: the guard can only withhold an offer, never add one, and the reader can still type the command. Recorded as out of the security boundary rather than as a finding.\n- Contradiction: none.\n- Partial success: the module cannot be exercised through the real advisor entry point without running the suite, which this lineage does not do; the exported functions were measured directly, which is sufficient for a cost and trust-boundary check.\n- Missing dependency: no secret or environment read exists to audit, so the absence was confirmed by reading the whole file rather than by a scan of one region.\n\n## Confirmed-Clean Surfaces\n\n- No import, `require`, `process.env`, filesystem or network access anywhere in `command-attach.ts`: the module reads only its arguments. Verified by scanning the whole file for import and environment forms, which returned nothing.\n- No prompt-controlled pattern: the `command` argument reaches the regex only through `escapePhrase` at line 316, and every caller passes a name from the generated tables (`attachCommand` line 527, `withholdMentionedCommands` at `fusion.ts:1212`, `typedCommandForTop` line 259). A prompt cannot contribute regex syntax.\n- No injection or escalation path: `commandOnlyMentioned` returns a boolean, and its only effect is to withhold an offer at `attachCommand:527` and to clear `passes_threshold` at `fusion.ts:1214`. A crafted prompt cannot make the advisor emit a command it would not have emitted for the same route without the guard.\n- No super-linear blow-up from the new patterns: a 100 KB prompt with one occurrence evaluates in 1.56 ms, a 25 KB or-list alternation in 1.24 ms, a 68 KB prompt whose tail repeats `and don't run it` 4,000 times in 2.08 ms, and 2,000 unled occurrences in 2.62 ms. The one costly shape, 2,000 led occurrences in 54 KB at 334 ms, is the pre-existing per-occurrence pattern construction that review pass twelve already measured and ruled out; the two new rules add one compile per occurrence to a loop that already built five.\n- No output, log or error path carries prompt text: the module has no logging and no throws on the prompt path.\n\n## Ruled Out\n\n- ReDoS in `COMMAND_DECLINED_BY_PRONOUN`: the pattern is an alternation of fixed literals with one bounded optional adverb group and no nested quantifier over the same text; the 68 KB repetition probe stays at 2 ms.\n- ReDoS in `COMMAND_AS_DOCUMENT`: the pattern is anchored at `^` and matches a fixed noun list, so it cannot backtrack across the prompt.\n- A homoglyph or zero-width bypass of the fold: a form that does not name a tracked command never matches `TRACKED_COMMANDS`, so no route selects a command, and the fold never makes a non-command match one.\n- A denial-of-service from the widened fold: dropping six more characters does not change the pass count, and every replace is a single global pass.\n\n## Next Focus\n\n- Dimension: maintainability.\n- Focus area: the comment block the diff reflowed and the readability of the new rules' documentation.\n- Reason: three dimensions are covered and the security pass is clean; maintainability is the last dimension and the diff visibly reflowed a comment paragraph.\n- Rotation status: correctness, traceability and security complete; maintainability last.\n- Blocked/productive carry-forward: productive `whole-file scan for imports and environment reads`; cheap and decisive for a clean security result.\n- Required evidence: line lengths and quoted text from the reflowed comment block.\n- Recovery note: none.\n\nReview verdict: CONDITIONAL\n","path":"specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/lineages/mention-13/iterations/iteration-003.md"},"output":"Created file successfully: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/lineages/mention-13/iterations/iteration-003.md","title":"write","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Created file successfully: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/lineages/mention-13/iterations/iteration-003.md"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"content\":\"# Review Iteration 003 - mention-13\\n\\n## Dispatcher\\n\\n- Mode: review. Target agent: `@deep-review`, executed in-process by this detached fan-out lineage (no nested dispatch).\\n- Session: `fanout-mention-13-1794865302020-qunumz`, generation 1, lineage `mention-13`.\\n- Iteration: 3 of 8. Dimension: security. Focus: the trust boundary of the prompt text, the two new patterns, and the module's import, environment and secret surface.\\n- Operator review focus, and how this iteration addressed it: pass thirteen's brief asks whether a request now reads as a mention and whether a refusal or question still reads as a request. This iteration asked the same questions from the trust side: whether a crafted prompt can make the guard offer a command it would not otherwise offer, or can make it spend unbounded work, and whether the widened fold opens an injection path. It found no security defect; the evidence is recorded below so the clean result is a checked result rather than an assumption.\\n\\n## Files Reviewed\\n\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:1-14` (module header, import surface)\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:197-245` (typed reader and its boundary constants)\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:316-476` (escaping, pattern construction, fold, occurrence scan)\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-attach-guards.vitest.ts:599-615` (generated-table contract tests)\\n\\n## Findings - New\\n\\n### P0 Findings\\n\\nNone.\\n\\n### P1 Findings\\n\\nNone new. F001 and F002 from iteration 1 remain active and are carried forward.\\n\\n### P2 Findings\\n\\nNone new.\\n\\n## Traceability Checks\\n\\n| Protocol | Status | Evidence |\\n|----------|--------|----------|\\n| `spec_code` | partial | Unchanged from iteration 2: the document bullet is narrower in code (F005). The security-relevant scope claim at `spec.md:165` (no holdout text read) was respected: this iteration read only source and test files. |\\n| `checklist_evidence` | partial | Unchanged from iteration 2: `CHK-FIX-002` overstates matrix coverage (F006). `NFR-P01` at `spec.md:162` claims a constant number of regex passes for the fold, which the fold satisfies: five `.replace` calls plus four single-pass regexes, none of them looped. |\\n| `skill_agent` | notApplicable | No agent definition in scope for this dimension. |\\n| `agent_cross_runtime` | notApplicable | No runtime mirror is touched. |\\n| `feature_catalog_code` | notApplicable | No feature catalog row covers a prompt-normalization security property. |\\n| `playbook_capability` | notApplicable | No playbook scenario exists for a security property of the mention guard. |\\n\\n## Integration Evidence\\n\\nNone this iteration: the trust-boundary review stayed inside the module and its consumer contract already cited in iteration 1.\\n\\n## Edge Cases\\n\\n- Ambiguity: whether a prompt that suppresses its own command offer is a security issue. It is not: the guard can only withhold an offer, never add one, and the reader can still type the command. Recorded as out of the security boundary rather than as a finding.\\n- Contradiction: none.\\n- Partial success: the module cannot be exercised through the real advisor entry point without running the suite, which this lineage does not do; the exported functions were measured directly, which is sufficient for a cost and trust-boundary check.\\n- Missing dependency: no secret or environment read exists to audit, so the absence was confirmed by reading the whole file rather than by a scan of one region.\\n\\n## Confirmed-Clean Surfaces\\n\\n- No import, `require`, `process.env`, filesystem or network access anywhere in `command-attach.ts`: the module reads only its arguments. Verified by scanning the whole file for import and environment forms, which returned nothing.\\n- No prompt-controlled pattern: the `command` argument reaches the regex only through `escapePhrase` at line 316, and every caller passes a name from the generated tables (`attachCommand` line 527, `withholdMentionedCommands` at `fusion.ts:1212`, `typedCommandForTop` line 259). A prompt cannot contribute regex syntax.\\n- No injection or escalation path: `commandOnlyMentioned` returns a boolean, and its only effect is to withhold an offer at `attachCommand:527` and to clear `passes_threshold` at `fusion.ts:1214`. A crafted prompt cannot make the advisor emit a command it would not have emitted for the same route without the guard.\\n- No super-linear blow-up from the new patterns: a 100 KB prompt with one occurrence evaluates in 1.56 ms, a 25 KB or-list alternation in 1.24 ms, a 68 KB prompt whose tail repeats `and don't run it` 4,000 times in 2.08 ms, and 2,000 unled occurrences in 2.62 ms. The one costly shape, 2,000 led occurrences in 54 KB at 334 ms, is the pre-existing per-occurrence pattern construction that review pass twelve already measured and ruled out; the two new rules add one compile per occurrence to a loop that already built five.\\n- No output, log or error path carries prompt text: the module has no logging and no throws on the prompt path.\\n\\n## Ruled Out\\n\\n- ReDoS in `COMMAND_DECLINED_BY_PRONOUN`: the pattern is an alternation of fixed literals with one bounded optional adverb group and no nested quantifier over the same text; the 68 KB repetition probe stays at 2 ms.\\n- ReDoS in `COMMAND_AS_DOCUMENT`: the pattern is anchored at `^` and matches a fixed noun list, so it cannot backtrack across the prompt.\\n- A homoglyph or zero-width bypass of the fold: a form that does not name a tracked command never matches `TRACKED_COMMANDS`, so no route selects a command, and the fold never makes a non-command match one.\\n- A denial-of-service from the widened fold: dropping six more characters does not change the pass count, and every replace is a single global pass.\\n\\n## Next Focus\\n\\n- Dimension: maintainability.\\n- Focus area: the comment block the diff reflowed and the readability of the new rules' documentation.\\n- Reason: three dimensions are covered and the security pass is clean; maintainability is the last dimension and the diff visibly reflowed a comment paragraph.\\n- Rotation status: correctness, traceability and security complete; maintainability last.\\n- Blocked/productive carry-forward: productive `whole-file scan for imports and environment reads`; cheap and decisive for a clean security result.\\n- Required evidence: line lengths and quoted text from the reflowed comment block.\\n- Recovery note: none.\\n\\nReview verdict: CONDITIONAL\\n\",\"path\":\"specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/lineages/mention-13/iterations/iteration-003.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794866428145,"end":1794866444457}}}}
